.TH YMX 1 "2026-09-04" "1.45" "ymx manual"
.SH NAME
ymx \\- YAML \\(r JSON compiler for the YMX language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ymx
[path] [flags]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ymx
is a YAML parser and compiler for the YMX language. It reads a YAML project
(a directory of .yml/.yaml files) and compiles the requested entry component to
JSON output.

A project is a directory containing YAML documents. Each top-level key-value
pair in a document defines a
.I component .
Components are callable with arguments, support templates (components whose
names start with $), and can reference each other via the \fBfrom\fR property
or inline $\fIcomp\fR(...) calls.

A project can span multiple files via the \fB_use\fR directive, which imports
components from other YAML files. Imported components become available in the
importing file's namespace, and transitively re-exported imports are available
to callers of the importing file.

The compiler applies rules 1\\(en22 of the YMX language. The output is a
JSON-encoded value (an object, array, string, number, bool, or null).

.SH "MULTI-FILE PROJECTS"

The \fB_use\fR directive controls which components are imported from other files
in the project. It is placed at the top level of a YAML document (before any
component definitions). Three forms are supported:

.SS "Bare wildcard (_use: *)"
The simplest form. When no \fB_use\fR is present, all sibling and sub-directory
files are automatically imported (recursive wildcard). Explicit \fB_use: *\fR
has the same effect.
.PP
.nf
    # main.yml \\(em imports everything from the project
    _use: *
    greeting: "Hello, $name"
.fi

.SS "Wildcard object (_use: {\"*\": \"file\"})"
Imports all public components from a specific file. The key \fB*\fR is a
wildcard matching all components in the named file.
.PP
.nf
    # main.yml \\(em imports all components from lib/utils.yml
    _use:
      "*": lib/utils
    greeting: $format_name($name)
.fi

.SS "Named import (_use: {alias: \"file.component\"})"
Imports a specific component under an alias. The alias becomes the local name
for the imported component.
.PP
.nf
    # main.yml \\(em imports 'helper' from lib/utils.yml as 'fmt'
    _use:
      fmt: lib/utils.helper
    result: $fmt($input)
.fi

.SS "Transitive re-export"
When file A imports file B, and file B itself has \fB_use\fR directives, B's
own imports are processed first. Names imported into B are then re-exportable
to A (and to callers of A). This makes import chains compose naturally.

.SS "File-scoped components"
Components whose names start with \fB_\fR (underscore) are file-scoped and
cannot be imported by other files. Attempting to reference them from outside
their document raises \fBE005\fR.

.SS "Cycle detection"
Circular \fB_use\fR chains are detected at load time and rejected with \fBE001\fR.

.SS "IPC declarations in _use"
When the right-hand side of a \fB_use\fR mapping entry is a mapping (not a
string), it declares an IPC component (rule 21). The mapping specifies the
external runner, transport, and protocol configuration. See the \fIRule 21\fR
section for details.
.PP
.nf
    _use:
      weather:
        runner: http
        url: "https://api.example.com/weather"
        method: GET
        response:
          path: "$.data"
.fi

.SH OPTIONS

These are the CLI flags supported by \fBymx\fR. For many flags, the entry
document\u\s-2\u\s0\s+2\u's \fB_ymx\fR front-matter block can supply a default
value; the precedence is \fICLI flag > entry-file _ymx > engine default\fR.

.SS "Positionals and entry"

.TP
.B path
The entry file or project directory. Required unless \fB--test\fR is given,
in which case it defaults to \fB.\fR (the current directory). When a directory
is given, it is treated as the project root. When a file is given, its parent
directory is the project root and the entry component is derived from the file
stem.

When \fBpath\fR is absent, \fBymx\fR reads the full stdin content as a YAML
script and writes it to a temporary \fBmain.yml\fR inside a temporary project
directory. The entry is \fBmain.main\fR (or \fB--entry\fR overrides it). A
terminal (tty) stdin with no \fBpath\fR produces a usage error (exit 2).

When \fBpath\fR is given, \fBymx\fR reads stdin as \fIcall arguments\fR for the
entry component: the stdin content is first parsed as JSON; if that fails it
is retried as YAML. The resulting value is converted to arguments:
object \fB{\&...}\fR becomes named arguments \fBname=value\fR,
array \fB[\&...]\fR becomes positional arguments \fB$0, $1, ...\fR,
and a scalar becomes a single positional argument \fB$0\fR.
A terminal stdin with \fBpath\fR given also produces a usage error (exit 2).
\fB--test\fR does not read stdin in either mode.
.TP
.B -e, --entry <component>
The name of the component within the entry file to compile (default:
\fBmain\fR). The entry path is derived internally as
\fI<file_stem>\fR.\fI<component>\fR (always exactly two segments: empty
folder.path + file stem + component). For example, \fBymx main.yml --entry
foo\fR compiles component \fBfoo\fR from \fBmain.yml\fR, with entry path
\fBmain.foo\fR.

.SS "Compiler flags"

.TP
.B -m, --max-depth <n>
Limit on template/call recursion (default: 256). Each recursive operation
(inline $\fIcomp\fR(...) call, math \fIcomp\fR(...) call, bare $name
component fallback, template step, \fBfrom\fR dispatch, brace call
(\fB$name{...}\fR), and IPC call) is checked against this cap. Exceeding
it raises \fBE008\fR.
.TP
.B -c, --code <yml>
Inline YAML/JSON component definition. The value is parsed as a YAML document
whose top-level keys become components. Can be repeated; later \fB-c\fR values
override earlier ones for the same component name. Example:
\fBymx -c 'greeting: "Hello"' main.yml\fR.
.TP
.B --plain
Promote all sub-namespace names (templates and regular components) into the
global namespace. Mutually exclusive with \fB--plain-template\fR. Overrides
\fB_ymx.plain\fR in the entry file.
.TP
.B --plain-template
Promote only template names (those starting with \fB$\fR) into the global
namespace. Regular components remain namespaced. Mutually exclusive with
\fB--plain\fR. Overrides \fB_ymx.plain\fR in the entry file.
.TP
.B -f, --format <json|diagnostics|compact>
Output format. \fBjson\fR (default) emits the compiled JSON value.
\fBcompact\fR emits compact JSON (no whitespace). \fBdiagnostics\fR emits
no JSON on success; on any diagnostic it lists each error to stderr.
.TP
.B --pretty
Pretty-print JSON output. Implies \fB--format json\fR and forces
indentation. Mutually exclusive with \fB--format compact\fR.

.SS "Execution and IPC"

.TP
.B --no-exec
Disable shell execution (rule 19). Any \fB$sh{...}\fR or \fB$pw{...}\fR
command raises \fBE016\fR. Useful in sandboxed or CI environments.
.TP
.B --allowed-backends <list>
Comma-separated list of allowed shell backends (default: all). Only the
listed backends may be used in \fB$sh{...}\fR / \fB$pw{...}\fR commands.
Unlisted backends raise \fBE016\fR.
.TP
.B --no-ipc
Disable IPC components (rule 21). Any IPC call raises \fBE018\fR. Useful
in environments without network access.
.TP
.B --allowed-ipc <list>
Comma-separated list of allowed IPC runner names (default: all). Only the
listed runners may be used in IPC declarations. Unlisted runners raise
\fBE018\fR.

.SS "Output and test"

.TP
.B -o, --output <file>
Write output to \fBfile\fR instead of stdout. The file is written only on
success; on any diagnostic the CLI exits non-zero \fBwithout creating the
file\fR.
.TP
.B -t, --test
Run inline \fB_test\fR blocks (via \fBymx-test\fR) instead of compiling the
entry component. When \fBpath\fR is omitted it defaults to \fB.\fR.
When \fBpath\fR is a directory, recursively walk it and run all \fB_test\fR
blocks in every subdirectory containing .yml/.yaml files (each is an
independent project); aggregate results across all projects and exit non-zero
if any test fails. When \fBpath\fR is a file, run tests for that single
project.
.TP
.B --errors
Show only errors. Suppresses successful JSON output; diagnostic messages are
written to stderr. Exit code is 0 only if no diagnostics were produced.

.SS "Help"

.TP
.B --help , -h
Print the short usage summary and exit 0. The full reference manual is
available in the man page: \fBman ymx\fR.

.SH RULES
The following rules define how YMX parses and resolves YAML into a JSON value.
Each rule is applied in order during compilation.

.SS "Rule 1 \\(em Top-level keys are components"
Every top-level key-value pair in a document defines a component.
The key is the component name; the value is its body.
.PP
.nf
    user:
      name: Mathew
      phone: 123456789
.fi
.PP
Here \fBuser\fR is a component with two properties.

.SS "Rule 2 \\(em Components are callable with arguments"
Arguments passed to a component are referenced inside its body by name
prefixed with \fB$\fR.
.PP
.nf
    user:
      name: $user_name
      phone: $user_phone
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBuser\fR with \fBuser_name=\"Alice\"\fR and \fBuser_phone=987654321\fR
produces \fB{\"name\": \"Alice\", \"phone\": 987654321}\fR.
.PP
Bare \fB$name\fR (no parentheses) resolves in order: (a) a named argument in
scope, (b) a regular component of that name called with no args, (c) error
(rule 10).

.SS "Rule 3 \\(em Inline call syntax ($comp(...))"
A \fB$name(...)\fR expression inside a value calls another component in place.
Positional and named arguments may be mixed; a positional arg may not follow a
named one (\fBE012\fR).
.PP
.nf
    a: $b(x=12, y=34)
    b: $x + $y
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBa\fR returns \fB\"12 + 34\"\fR.
.PP
.nf
    a: $b(12, y=34)    # positional $0=12, named y=34
    b: $0 * $y
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBa\fR returns \fB408\fR.

.SS "Rule 4 \\(em Positional arguments ($0, $1, ...)"
When calling a component with \fB$\fR, unnamed arguments bind to positional
slots \fB$0\fR, \fB$1\fR, \fB$2\fR, ...
.PP
.nf
    a: $b(12, 34)
    b: $0 + $1
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBa\fR returns \fB\"12 + 34\"\fR.
.PP
Integer YAML keys denote positional slots: \fB0:\fR sets \fB$0\fR,
\fB1:\fR sets \fB$1\fR, etc.

.SS "Rule 5 \\(em Template chaining ($a \\(-> $$a \\(-> $$$a)"
A component whose name starts with \fB$\fR is a template and is applied
automatically after its matching (non-\fB$\fR) component is called.
Templates can be chained indefinitely: \fB$a\fR, \fB$$a\fR, \fB$$$a\fR, ...
Each link runs in order; the innermost feeds the next.
.PP
.nf
    $box:
      from: div
      children: Hello, $name!
    box:
      name: Sir. $name
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBbox\fR with \fBname=\"Rocky\"\fR produces
\fB{\"from\": \"div\", \"children\": \"Hello, Sir. Rocky\"}\fR.
.PP
Chain example:
.PP
.nf
    $$$a: "final: $0"
    $$a:  $0 + 1
    $a:   $0 * 2
    a:    10
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBa\fR: 10 \\(-> 20 \\(-> 21 \\(-> \fB\"final: 21\"\fR.
.PP
Templates are namespaced by default: \fB$box\fR in \fBsubdir/\fR applies only
to components in the \fBsubdir\fR namespace. Use \fB_ymx.plain: true\fR
to promote sub-namespace names globally.

.SS "Rule 6 \\(em from property for explicit dispatch"
The \fBfrom\fR property references another component by name, which is called
with the current component's remaining properties as arguments.
Override the keyword with \fB--from-keyword\fR or \fB_ymx.from_keyword\fR.
.PP
.nf
    CompA:
      from: CompB
      x: 12
      y: 34
    CompB: $x + $y
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBCompA\fR returns \fB\"12 + 34\"\fR.
.PP
If \fBfrom\fR does not resolve to a valid regular component, it is treated as
a plain property (no error).

.SS "Rule 7 \\(em Math expressions in call arguments"
\fB${...}\fR evaluates its contents as a math expression.
Component calls inside math use \fBname(...)\fR syntax (no leading \fB$\fR).
Math operators: \fB+\fR (add/concat), \fB-\fR, \fB*\\fR, \fB/\\fR (float),
\fB%\\fR (mod), \fB**\\fR (pow). Precedence: \fB**\\fR > unary \fB-\fR >
\fB* / %\fR > \fB+ -\fR.
.PP
.nf
    a: $b(12, 34)
    b: ${$0 + $1}
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBa\fR returns the number \fB46\fR.
.PP
.nf
    a: ${b(12,34) + c(28)}
    b: ${$0 + $1}
    c: ${2 * $0}
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBa\fR returns \fB102\fR.
.PP
String operands are re-scanned as math expressions when possible.
\fB\"1 + 2\"\fR as a string operand evaluates to \fB3\fR.
Identifiers inside \fB${...}\fR (without \fB$\fR) are in-scope arguments or
\fBlast\fR (previous reduce result). Use \fB$0\fR, \fB$1\fR for positional.
Use \fBname(...)\fR (no \fB$\fR) to call a component; dotted paths allowed
(e.g. \fBsubdir.comp(...)\fR).

.SS "Rule 8 \\(em Shortcut: property name matching a component calls it"
If a component defines a property whose name matches another component, that
property value is passed as \fB$0\fR to the matched component, and the
remaining properties are passed as named arguments.
.PP
.nf
    a:
      b: 1
      y: 3
      z: 5
    b: [$0, $y, $z]
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBa\fR returns \fB[1, 3, 5]\fR.
.PP
Shortcut fires during step 3 of rule 11. It is suppressed when \fBfrom\fR
points to a valid regular component.

.SS "Rule 9 \\(em Non-existing properties are ignored"
Extra caller properties that a component does not reference are silently
ignored.
.PP
.nf
    a: $x + $y
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBa\fR with \fB{\"x\":1,\"y\":2,\"z\":3}\fR returns \fB\"1 + 2\"\fR;
\fBz\fR is ignored.

.SS "Rule 10 \\(em All referenced properties are required"
Any property referenced via \fB$name\fR, \fB$0\fR, \fB${name}\fR, etc.
must be supplied by the caller or declared optional (rule 17).
Missing a required argument raises \fBE003\fR.

.SS "Rule 11 \\(em Three-step resolution order"
Resolving a component runs in three fixed steps:
.TP
1.
\fBProperty resolution (before template)\fR \\(em every property value is fully
resolved. Nested call-sites (\fB$comp(...)\fR, \fB${...}\fR) resolve
bottom-up. Bare \fB$name\fR resolves as: argument \\(-> component call \\(->
error. Property-key modifiers (\fB?\fR, \fB$\\fR) are stripped first.
.TP
2.
\fBTemplate chain (rule 5)\fR \\(em applied to the post-step-1 result.
.TP
3.
\fBfrom / shortcut dispatch (rules 6 and 8)\fR \\(em mutually exclusive;
\fBfrom\fR takes priority if valid, otherwise the shortcut fires.

.SS "Rule 12 \\(em Array template: map over items"
When a component returns an array and a matching \fB$template\fR exists,
each array item is passed through the template individually.
.PP
.nf
    $a:
      prop1: ${x + 1}
      prop2: ${y * x}
    a:
      - x: 1
        y: 2
      - x: 3
        y: 4
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBa\fR produces
\fB[{\"prop1\": 2, \"prop2\": 2}, {\"prop1\": 4, \"prop2\": 12}]\fR.

.SS "Rule 13 \\(em Array template reduces to last application result"
When the template is an array, it iterates over its items, carrying the
previous result as \fB$last\fR. The final result is the return value of the
last iteration.
.PP
Each step starts from the \fBinital\fR arguments; the previous step's result
only overwrites keys it actually returns, and only for the immediately next
step.
.PP
.nf
    a:
      x: 1
      y: 2
    $a:
      - x: ${x + 1}
        y: ${y + 2}
      - ${x + y}
      - $x + $y < $last
.fi
.PP
Calling \fBa\fR:
.IP 1. 3
Step 1: \fBx=1, y=2\fR \\(-> \fB{\"x\": 2, \"y\": 4}\fR (object returned)
.IP 2. 3
Step 2: \fBx=2, y=4\fR \\(-> \fB6\fR (scalar; no overwrite carries forward)
.IP 3. 3
Step 3: reverts to \fBx=1, y=2, $last=6\fR \\(-> \fB\"1 + 2 < 6\"\fR
.PP
Result: \fB\"1 + 2 < 6\"\fR.

.SS "Rule 14 \\(em Both-array reduce"
When both \fBa\fR and \fB$a\fR are arrays, \fB$a\fR is applied as a reduce
sequence independently to each element of \fBa\fR. Result is an array of
reduced entries.
.PP
.nf
    $a:
      - {sum: ${x + y}, x: 0}
      - ${sum + x + y}
      - a: $x
        b: ${2*y}
        sum: $last
    a:
      - x: 1
        y: 2
      - x: 3
        y: 4
.fi
.PP
Result: \fB[{\"a\": 1, \"b\": 4, \"sum\": 5}, {\"a\": 3, \"b\": 8, \"sum\": 11}]\fR.

.SS "Rule 15 \\(em $merge builtin"
\fB$merge(a, b)\fR merges two values. Arrays are concatenated; objects are
shallow-merged (second overwrites first for shared keys). Both arguments are
evaluated eagerly. Mixed shapes are \fBE011\fR.
.PP
.nf
    a: [1, 2, 3]
    b: [4, 5, 6]
    c: $merge(a, b)
    # c -> [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
.fi
.PP
.nf
    defaults: {theme: dark, lang: en}
    overrides: {debug: true, lang: fr}
    config: $merge(defaults, overrides)
    # config -> {theme: dark, lang: fr, debug: true}
.fi

.SS "Rule 16 \\(em $map and $reduce builtins"
.TP
\fB$map(callable, array)\fR \\(em applies \fBcallable\fR to each item of
\fBarray\fR, returning an array of results. First arg (callable) is kept
unevaluated; second arg is evaluated eagerly. Object items supply named args;
scalar items bind \fB$0\fR.
.PP
.nf
    double: ${$0 * 2}
    items: [1, 2, 3]
    result: $map($double, $items)
    # result -> [2, 4, 6]
.fi
.PP
.TP
\fB$reduce(callable, array)\fR \\(em applies \fBcallable\fR iteratively over
\fBarray\fR, accumulating a result. The last iteration's return value is the
result; empty array yields \fBnull\fR. First arg unevaluated; second arg eager.
\fB$last\fR holds the previous result (undefined on first step; \fBE003\fR if
referenced there).
.PP
.nf
    sum_step: $a + $b
    items:
      - {a: 10, b: 0}
      - {a: 20, b: 10}
      - {a: 30, b: 30}
    result: $reduce($sum_step, $items)
    # result -> "30 + 30"
.fi

.SS "Rule 17 \\(em ? optional property-key modifier (v1)"
A property key of the form \fB<name>?:\fR declares an optional property with
a default value. The caller-supplied value wins; if absent, the default is
used. \fB?:\fR relaxes rule 10 for that property.
.PP
.nf
    $a:
      x?: 2
    a:
      x: 1
    # Calling a -> {"x": 1}   (caller wins)
    # Calling a with no x -> {"x": 2}   (default)
.fi
.PP
\fB<name>?$:\fR combines optional with math evaluation of the default:
.PP
.nf
    $button:
      label?$: "Click me"
      count?$: count + 1
      disabled: false
    counter:
      count: 0
    main: $button
    # -> {label: "Click me", count: 1, disabled: false}
.fi
.PP
Merge mode activates only when the callee body contains at least one \fB?:\fR
property. In merge mode, caller-supplied keys forward into the output and win
over the body; the body (including \fB?:\fR defaults) fills gaps.

.SS "Rule 18 \\(em $ math shorthand property-key modifier (v2)"
A trailing \fB$\fR on a property key wraps the value in \fB${...}\fR.
\fB<name>$: <src>\fR is equivalent to \fB<name>: ${<src>}\fR.
.PP
.nf
    a:
      sum$: x + y
    # same as: a: {sum: ${x + y}}
.fi
.PP
On a component name: \fBa$: x + y\fR is equivalent to \fBa: ${x + y}\fR.
.PP
Combined with \fB?\fR: \fB<name>?$: <src>\fR is equivalent to
\fB<name>?: ${<src>}\fR (optional with math-evaluated default).
\fB<name>$?:\fR is \fBE010\fR (wrong order).
.PP
Note: rule 18 is \fBv2\fR. In v1, \fB$\fR on a property key or the
wrong-order modifier combination \fB$?:\fR surfaces as \fBE010\fR.

.SS "Rule 19 \\(em Shell components (sh, pw)"
Shell components execute an external command and return its output.
.TP
\fB$sh{cmd}\fR \\(em runs \fBcmd\fR via the system shell (\fB/bin/sh -c\fR).
.TP
\fB$pw{cmd}\fR \\(em runs \fBcmd\fR via a persistent shell (keeps state across
calls within the same session).
.PP
Property-key shorthand: \fBmain$sh: cmd\fR is equivalent to
\fBmain: $sh{cmd}\fR.
.PP
The result is an object with two fields:
.TP
.B exit_code
The integer exit code of the process (0 on success).
.TP
.B stdout
The captured standard output as a string.
.PP
.nf
    result: $sh{echo hello}
    # result -> {exit_code: 0, stdout: "hello"}
.fi
.PP
Shell backends (\fBsh\fR, \fBpw\fR) are reserved component names (\fBE007\fR).
Shell execution is restricted by \fB_ymx.allowed_backends\fR and the
\fB--allowed-backends\fR CLI flag. If the requested backend is not in the
allowed list, or if no executor is provided (e.g. \fB--no-exec\fR), the
command raises \fBE016\fR. Spawn failures also raise \fBE016\fR.

.SS "Rule 20 \\(em HTML renderer"
When a component resolves to an HTML rendering context, the \fBfrom\fR property
selects the HTML tag, and \fBchildren\fR provides the content.
.TP
.B from: <tag>
Literal tag syntax: \fBfrom: <div>\fR renders an HTML \fB<div>\fR element
(not a component reference).
.TP
.B children
Content inside the tag. Can be:
.RS
.IP \\(bu 2
A scalar string (rendered as text content)
.IP \\(bu 2
An object with a \fBfrom\fR key (nested element)
.IP \\(bu 2
\fBnull\fR (empty element)
.RE
.PP
.nf
    card:
      from: <div>
      class: card
      style: "padding: 1rem"
      children:
        from: <h1>
        children: "Hello"
.fi
.PP
Shorthand: \fB<div>: value\fR is equivalent to
\fB{from: <div>, children: value}\fR.
.PP
\fBclass\fR and \fBstyle\fR properties are normalized: a scalar string
value is kept as-is; an array of strings is joined with spaces.
.PP
Array or object children without a \fBfrom\fR wrapper raise \fBE017\fR.

.SS "Rule 21 \\(em External components via _use IPC declarations"
IPC (Inter-Process Communication) components call external services declared
in \fB_use\fR mappings. When the right-hand side of a \fB_use\fR entry is a
mapping (not a string), it declares an IPC component.
.PP
Configuration fields:
.TS
tab(@);
lB lB l.
Field@Type@Description
_
runner@string@IPC backend: "http", "grpc", "cli", "process"
transport@string@Transport: "http", "stdio", "unix", "tcp"
protocol@protocol@protocol config (HTTP method/url, gRPC service/proto, etc.)
request@mapping@Request shaping: headers, body template, query params
response@mapping@Response mapping: JSON path extraction, status code checks
http@mapping@HTTP-specific: follow_redirects, tls_verify, timeout_ms
timeout@integer@Overall call timeout in milliseconds
lifecycle@mapping@Hooks: on_request, on_response, on_error
.TE
.PP
Wire format is determined by the \fBmode\fR field:
.TP
.B mode: text
The request body is a plain text string; the response is parsed as text.
.TP
.B mode: json
The request body is JSON-encoded; the response is parsed as JSON.
.PP
IPC sessions are created per-call by default. A session persists across
multiple calls when \fBlifetime: session\fR is set in the config.
.PP
IPC call failures (no host, disallowed transport, spawn failure, crash,
protocol error, timeout, error_pattern match, non-2xx HTTP) raise \fBE018\fR.
.PP
.nf
    _use:
      weather:
        runner: http
        url: "https://api.example.com/weather"
        method: GET
        mode: json
        response:
          path: "$.data"
    forecast: $weather({city: "London"})
.fi

.SS "Rule 22 \\(em Brace calls and the $<name> key suffix"
Brace call syntax provides a concise way to call a component with a payload.
.TP
.B $name{payload}
Calls component \fBname\fR with \fBpayload\fR. The payload is interpreted as:
.RS
.IP \\(bu 2
\fIScalar\fR \\(em bound to \fB$0\fR (single positional argument)
.IP \\(bu 2
\fIObject\fR \\(em each key-value pair becomes a named argument
.IP \\(bu 2
\fIArray\fR \\(em each element becomes a positional argument (\fB$0\fR, \fB$1\fR, ...)
.IP \\(bu 2
\fIEmpty\fR \\(em no arguments passed
.RE
.PP
.nf
    greet: $hello{world}         # $0 = "world"
    add: $sum({x: 1, y: 2})     # x=1, y=2
    pair: $zip([1, 2], [3, 4])  # $0=[1,2], $1=[3,4]
    noop: $done{}                # no arguments
.fi
.PP
Key suffix form: \fBkey$name: value\fR is equivalent to
\fBkey: $name{value}\fR.
.PP
.nf
    result$sh: echo hello
    # equivalent to: result: $sh{echo hello}
.fi
.PP
Brace calls are equivalent to \fBfrom\fR dispatch and shortcut forms where
applicable. Brace calls and IPC calls count as recursive operations for the
purposes of \fB--max-depth\fR (rule 8).

.SH "EXIT CODES"

.TP
.B 0
Success. For \fB--format diagnostics\fR this also means no diagnostics were
produced; stdout is empty.
.TP
.B 1
A diagnostic was produced: a load-time error (YAML parse failure,
duplicate component, reserved name, I/O failure), an option-resolution error
(missing entry, invalid _ymx field), or a compile-time error (unknown
component, missing argument, max-depth exceeded, type error, etc.). The
\fB--output\fR file is not created when exiting with code 1.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: unknown flag, missing required argument, wrong number of
positional arguments, or stdin is a terminal when it must be a pipe.

.SH EXAMPLES

Compile \fBmain.yml\fR and emit JSON to stdout:
.PP
.nf
    ymx main.yml
.fi
.PP

Compile a specific component:
.PP
.nf
    ymx main.yml --entry foo
.fi
.PP

Pretty-print JSON output:
.PP
.nf
    ymx main.yml --pretty
.fi
.PP

Write output to a file (only created on success):
.PP
.nf
    ymx main.yml --output out.json
.fi
.PP

Compile with a custom recursion limit:
.PP
.nf
    ymx main.yml --max-depth 128
.fi
.PP

Run inline tests for a project:
.PP
.nf
    ymx . --test
.fi
.PP

Emit diagnostics instead of JSON (useful in CI):
.PP
.nf
    ymx main.yml --format diagnostics
.fi
.PP

Compile a YAML script from stdin (equivalent to ymx main.yml):
.PP
.nf
    cat main.yml | ymx
.fi
.PP

Pass call arguments via stdin (calls component 'main' with x=1):
.PP
.nf
    echo '{"x":1}' | ymx main.yml
.fi
.PP

Pass positional arguments via stdin (binds $0=[1, 2, 3]):
.PP
.nf
    echo '[1, 2, 3]' | ymx main.yml
.fi
.PP

YAML arguments (when JSON parse fails, YAML is tried):
.PP
.nf
    echo 'x: 1' | ymx main.yml
.fi
.PP

Select a different entry component with stdin args:
.PP
.nf
    echo '{"a":1}' | ymx --entry foo main.yml
.fi
.PP

Multi-file project with _use:
.PP
.nf
    # Project structure:
    #   main.yml      \\(em entry point
    #   lib/utils.yml \\(em shared components
    #
    # main.yml:
    _use:
      "*": lib/utils
    greeting: $format_name($name)
.fi
.PP

Named import and rename:
.PP
.nf
    # main.yml \\(em import 'helper' as 'fmt'
    _use:
      fmt: lib/utils.helper
    result: $fmt($input)
.fi
.PP

Inline code with -c:
.PP
.nf
    ymx main.yml -c 'greeting: "Hello from inline"'
.fi
.PP

-c with file overlay (inline overrides file components):
.PP
.nf
    ymx main.yml -c 'override: "this wins"'
.fi
.PP

Shell execution:
.PP
.nf
    ymx main.yml -c 'result: $sh{echo hello}'
.fi
.PP

IPC declaration in _use:
.PP
.nf
    # main.yml
    _use:
      weather:
        runner: http
        url: "https://api.example.com/weather"
        method: GET
    forecast: $weather
.fi
.PP

HTML rendering with literal tags:
.PP
.nf
    ymx main.yml -c '
    page:
      from: <html>
      children:
        from: <body>
        children:
          from: <h1>
          children: "Hello, World!"
    '
.fi
.PP

.SH "PROJECT METADATA"

.SS "_ymx \\(em front matter"
The \fB_ymx\fR key in the entry file's YAML document provides compiler
options. Precedence: \fICLI flag > entry-file _ymx > engine default\fR.
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lB lB lB l.
Field@Type@Default@Notes
_
max_depth@integer@256@Recursion cap for templates, calls, brace calls, IPC
from_keyword@string@from@Override the dispatch keyword
format@string@json@Output format: json, compact, diagnostics
pretty@boolean@false@Pretty-print JSON (implies format json)
plain@enum@namespaced@Template promotion: namespaced, true, false
allowed_backends@list@[sh,pw]@Allowed shell backends
allowed_ipc@list@all@Allowed IPC runner names (empty = allow all)
.TE
.PP

.SS "_test \\(em inline tests"
The \fB_test\fR key defines inline test cases for a component. Three forms:
.TP
.B Form A \\(em simple value
\fB_test: expected_json\fR \\(em the component must compile to this value.
.TP
.B Form B-value \\(em expected value
\fB_test:\fR with \fBtarget: component_name\fR and \fBexpected: value\fR.
.TP
.B Form B-error \\(em expected error
\fB_test:\fR with \fBtarget: component_name\fR and \fBerror: diagnostic_code\fR.

.SS "_use \\(em file imports"
See \fIMULTI-FILE PROJECTS\fR section above for the full \fB_use\fR directive
documentation, including wildcard imports, named imports, transitive re-export,
and IPC declarations.

.SH "SHELL COMPLETION"

Shell completion scripts are provided in the \fBcompletions/\fR directory:

.TP
.B completions/ymx.bash
Bash completion. Source from \fB~/.bashrc\fR or
\fB/etc/bash_completion.d/ymx.bash\fR.
.TP
.B completions/_ymx
Zsh completion. Place in \fB~/.zsh/completions/\fR or a directory in
\fB$fpath\fR.
.TP
.B completions/ymx.fish
Fish completion. Place in \fB~/.config/fish/completions/\fR.

.SH DIAGNOSTICS

Every diagnostic renders to stderr as:
.PP
.nf
    [\fIcode\fR] \fIfile\fR:\fIline\fR:\fIcol\fR (\fIcomponent\fR): \fImessage\fR
.fi
.PP

The following error codes are defined:

.TS
tab(@);
lB lB l.
Code@Stage@Description
_
E001@load@YAML parse error or unsupported YAML feature. Multi-document streams (---),
 complex mapping keys, YAML merge keys (<<), or non-string top-level keys are
 not supported. Also the catch-all for I/O failures (missing root, unreadable
 file). Cyclic _use chains also raise E001.
E002@compile@Unknown component reference. A $name or ${name} or from: name
 refers to a component that does not exist in any reachable namespace.
E003@compile@Missing required argument. A property $x is referenced but neither
 supplied by the caller nor declared with x?: (optional).
E004@load@Duplicate component name in the same namespace. Two top-level
 definitions of the same component in files belonging to the same namespace.
E005@compile@File-scope violation. A _-prefixed (file-scoped) component is
 referenced from outside its document, or is imported via _use.
E006@compile@Ambiguous shortcut. Two properties in the same component body both
 match names of existing components, making the rule-8 shortcut ambiguous.
E007@load@Reserved builtin name. Defining a component or template named map,
 reduce, merge, sh, or pw (or $map, $$reduce, etc.) is rejected.
E008@compile@Max-depth exceeded. The recursion depth cap (--max-depth, default
 256) was exhausted. Brace calls and IPC calls count as recursive operations.
E009@options@Entry not found. The entry path has fewer than two segments,
 resolves to a missing file, has an ambiguous .yml/.yaml stem, or the entry
 name is not a valid component identifier.
E010@both@Invalid syntax. Malformed call-site, bad string escape (\\X), math
 identifier prefix ($letter), mixed-shape template chain, unknown/invalid _ymx
 field, or malformed _test block. Also the v1 rejection of rule-17/18
 constructs (?: property-key optional syntax, $ suffix on property key).
E011@compile@Math error or builtin argument type error. Division by zero,
 non-numeric operand, type mismatch in math, non-array second argument to
 $map/$reduce, or mixed-shape $merge.
E012@compile@Positional argument after a named argument. $f(name=1, 2) is
 not allowed.
E013@compile@Array or object literal as a direct call argument. $f([1,2,3])
 or $f({a:1}) is not supported in v1 via call syntax. Note: the brace form
 (rule 22) accepts array and object payloads.
E015@load@Meta-key reserved name. A top-level key that is a leading-$ variant
 of _ymx or _test (e.g. $_ymx, $$_test) is rejected as reserved.
E016@compile@Shell execution error. The executor was not provided (--no-exec),
 the requested backend is not in the allowed list (--allowed-backends), or
 the command failed to spawn.
E017@render@Array or object children without a from component wrapper. HTML
 children must be scalar, an object with a from key, or null.
E018@compile@IPC call failure. No host provided, disallowed transport
 (--no-ipc / --allowed-ipc), spawn failure, process crash, protocol error,
 timeout, error_pattern match, or non-2xx HTTP status.
.TE

